def section(title,content):return f'<section class="case-body"><h2>{title}</h2><div>{content}</div></section>'
def evidence(title,detail):return f'<div class="finding"><strong>{title}</strong><p>{detail}</p></div>'
def case(p,dash,findings,method,limits,files):
 s=p['slug'];body=f'''<section class="case-hero"><div class="crumb"><a href="projects.html">All projects</a> / {p['title']}</div><span class="eyebrow">Independent public-data project · {p['tools']}</span><h1>{p['question']}</h1><p class="lead">{p['deliverable']}</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="#dashboard">Try the dashboard</a><a class="button secondary" href="{BASE}/tree/main/{s}">View code &amp; results</a></div><div class="case-facts"><div><strong>Data</strong>{p['data']}</div><div><strong>Scope</strong>{p['scope']}</div><div><strong>Role</strong>Analysis · Modeling · Product design</div></div></section><section class="dashboard" id="dashboard"><div class="dash-title"><div><h2>{p['title']}</h2><p>Change the controls to explore the computed results.</p></div><span class="live-badge">Interactive analysis</span></div><div id="interactive">{dash}</div><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to use the dashboard, or download the CSV files below.</p></noscript></section>'''
 body+=section('The problem',f'<p>{p["problem"]}</p><p><strong>The decision:</strong> {p["decision"]}</p>')
 body+=section('What I built',f'<p>{p["deliverable"]}</p><div class="flow"><span>Public source</span><span>Validate &amp; prepare</span><span>Analyze &amp; test</span><span>Explore &amp; decide</span></div>')
 body+=section('What the evidence says',findings)
 body+=section('How it works',method)
 body+=section('Limits &amp; next steps',limits)
 body+=section('Open the project',f'<p>Reproduce the analysis, inspect the results, and check the data definitions.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="{BASE}/tree/main/{s}">Project on GitHub</a><a class="button secondary" href="{s}/README.md">Read the project notes</a></div><ul>'+''.join(f'<li><a href="{s}/{f}" download>{label}</a></li>' for f,label in files)+'</ul>')
 body+=f'<div class="actions" style="margin-bottom:55px"><a class="button secondary" href="projects.html">Explore the other projects</a></div>'
 page(s+'.html',p['title'],body,'Projects',s)

revenue_dash='''<div class="controls"><div class="field"><label for="region">Customer market</label><select id="region"><option>All</option><option>United Kingdom</option><option>International</option></select></div><div class="field"><label for="metric">Chart metric</label><select id="metric"><option value="net_sales">Net merchandise sales</option><option value="gross_sales">Gross merchandise sales</option><option value="credit_value">Credits</option></select></div></div><div class="kpis" id="kpis" aria-live="polite"></div><div class="chart-panel"><h3 id="chart-title">Monthly sales</h3><div id="chart"></div><p class="subtle">Horizontal axis: month of 2011. Vertical axis: GBP.</p></div><p class="note" id="region-note"></p><button class="download" id="download-data">Download filtered monthly data</button><details><summary>View exact monthly values</summary><div id="data-table"></div></details><div class="dash-bottom"><section><h3 class="small-heading">Customer segments · all markets</h3><p class="subtle">Identified customers only. Active means purchased within 60 days of 1 Dec 2011; repeat means at least two orders in the window.</p><div id="segments"></div></section><section><h3 class="small-heading">Observed customer cohorts · all markets</h3><div class="field"><label for="cohort">First observed purchase month</label><select id="cohort"></select></div><div id="cohort-results"></div><p class="subtle">Month 0 is cohort size. Later months are observed repeat purchasing, not proof of true acquisition or lifetime retention.</p></section></div>'''
rf=evidence(f'{money(R["net_sales"])} in net merchandise sales',f'{money(R["gross_sales"])} in positive sales less {money(R["credit_value"])} in credit lines across complete January–November months.')+evidence(f'{pct(R["repeat_share"])} observed repeat-buyer share',f'{fmt(R["known_customers"])} identified customers; only {pct(R["identified_revenue_share"])} of gross sales can be linked to a customer ID. Anonymous purchases are retained in revenue reporting.')+evidence('A lifecycle experiment, with a clear denominator','Use active and lapsed repeat-buyer segments to design different outreach tests. Measure incremental net revenue and unsubscribe rates in a randomized experiment; no retention uplift is claimed here.')
rm='''<ol><li>Load the official UCI workbook and verify its archive checksum. Audit missing customer IDs and remove exact duplicate invoice lines.</li><li>Keep product-like stock codes, nonzero quantities, and positive unit prices. Service and manual adjustment codes are excluded. Credits remain signed; positive non-cancelled lines form the purchase population.</li><li>Restrict reporting to complete Jan–Nov 2011 months. Calculate monthly sales in SQL and reconcile the totals against Python.</li><li>Use identified positive-sale customers for order frequency, recency segments, and cohorts. Preserve anonymous sales in the revenue denominator.</li></ol>'''
rl='''<p>Revenue is merchandise value, not profit. Credit notes are not reliably matched to their original sale, so credit value divided by gross sales is not a product-return rate. Exact-duplicate removal assumes repeated invoice lines are accidental; legitimate repeated lines could be removed. Bulk orders and unusual quantities are retained and should be investigated.</p><p>The dataset is historical, covers one retailer, and includes wholesale behavior. Customer IDs are missing on many rows; first observed purchase is not necessarily the customer’s first purchase. A production version needs order identifiers, margin, attribution, and validated credit matching.</p><p>Source: <a href="https://doi.org/10.24432/C5BW33">Chen (2015), UCI Online Retail</a>, CC BY 4.0. These are portfolio findings, not results from an employer engagement.</p>'''
case(projects[0],revenue_dash,rf,rm,rl,[('monthly-revenue.csv','Monthly revenue and credits (CSV)'),('customer-segments.csv','Customer segments (CSV)'),('cohort-retention.csv','Observed cohort retention (CSV)'),('metrics.sql','Executed SQL definitions'),('analysis.py','Reproducible Python analysis'),('results.json','Full computed results (JSON)')])
forecast_dash='''<div class="controls"><div class="field"><label for="sku">Product</label><select id="sku"></select></div></div><div class="kpis" id="kpis" aria-live="polite"></div><div class="chart-panel"><h3>November 2011 · one-day-ahead unit sales</h3><div id="chart"></div><div class="legend"><span class="actual">Actual units</span><span>Selected forecast</span><span class="baseline">Weekly baseline</span></div></div><p class="note" id="method-note"></p><button class="download" id="download-data">Download selected backtest</button><details><summary>View exact daily values</summary><div id="data-table"></div></details><div class="dash-bottom"><section><h3 class="small-heading">Explore a planning assumption</h3><p class="subtle">A transparent coverage calculation, using average held-out forecasts.</p><div class="controls"><div class="field"><label for="lead">Coverage days</label><select id="lead"><option>3</option><option selected>7</option><option>14</option></select></div><div class="field"><label for="buffer">Extra buffer</label><select id="buffer"><option value="0">0%</option><option value="20" selected>20%</option><option value="40">40%</option></select></div></div></section><section><h3 id="plan-result"></h3><p class="subtle" id="plan-note"></p><p class="subtle">Does not include current stock, open orders, lead-time uncertainty, or service-level targets.</p></section></div>'''
ff=evidence(f'{pct(F["mae_change"])} lower average error than the weekly baseline',f'The validation-selected methods achieved {F["selected"]["mae"]:.1f} units mean absolute error versus {F["baseline"]["mae"]:.1f} on 150 held-out SKU-days.')+evidence(f'Machine learning selected for {F["ml_selected"]} of 5 products','October validation chose a method per product. The remaining products kept last week’s sales as their forecast. November was held out until evaluation.')+evidence('The forecast is still too uncertain to automate replenishment',f'Weighted absolute percentage error is {pct(F["selected"]["wape"])}. Investigate bulk-order spikes and test additional periods before using these forecasts operationally.')
fm='''<ol><li>Select the five most frequently ordered UK products using data before October only.</li><li>Create a daily unit-sales series through November. Days without recorded sales are zero; no stock-availability data is available.</li><li>Build lagged values at 1, 7, 14 and 28 days, shifted rolling means, and known calendar features. Every feature precedes its target.</li><li>Train gradient boosting through September; compare it with a 7-day seasonal baseline on October MAE.</li><li>Freeze the chosen method, refit using data through October, and evaluate one-day-ahead predictions in November. Earlier actuals may be used as each day arrives; this is not a 30-day-ahead forecast.</li></ol>'''
fl='''<p>These are observed sales, not unconstrained demand. Stockouts, price changes, promotions and wholesale orders are not modeled. Only five products and one test month are evaluated; selection by order frequency does not represent the full catalog.</p><p>The all-product gradient-boosting result is also reported in the repository. It performs better on November, but choosing it after seeing that holdout would invalidate the selection process. The dashboard keeps the method chosen on October validation.</p><p>Next: rolling-origin evaluation across seasons, prediction intervals, inventory data, and a monitored planner pilot. Source: <a href="https://doi.org/10.24432/C5BW33">Chen (2015), UCI Online Retail</a>, CC BY 4.0. No inventory savings or stockout reduction is claimed.</p>'''
case(projects[1],forecast_dash,ff,fm,fl,[('backtest.csv','All held-out forecasts (CSV)'),('results.json','Model comparison and evaluation (JSON)'),('analysis.py','Forecasting pipeline (Python)')])
conversion_dash='''<div class="controls"><div class="field"><label for="threshold">Decision threshold: <output id="threshold-value">0.25</output></label><input class="threshold-input" id="threshold" type="range" min="0.05" max="0.95" step="0.05" value="0.25" aria-describedby="threshold-note"></div></div><div class="kpis" id="kpis" aria-live="polite"></div><div class="chart-panel"><h3>Precision and recall · November–December holdout</h3><div id="chart"></div><div class="legend"><span>Precision</span><span class="baseline">Recall</span></div><p class="subtle">Horizontal axis: score threshold. Vertical axis: precision or recall.</p></div><p class="note" id="threshold-note" role="status"></p><div class="dash-bottom"><section><h3 class="small-heading">What happens at this threshold?</h3><div class="matrix" id="matrix"></div></section><section><h3 class="small-heading">Which signals does the model use?</h3><p class="subtle">Drop in average precision when a feature is shuffled. Association, not causation.</p><div id="importance"></div></section></div><button class="download" id="download-data">Download threshold results</button><details><summary>View the full threshold table</summary><div id="data-table"></div></details>'''
cf=evidence(f'{C["test"]["average_precision"]:.3f} average precision on unseen months',f'Compared with a {C["baseline"]["average_precision"]:.3f} constant-score baseline, on {fmt(C["test_sessions"])} sessions. ROC AUC is {C["test"]["roc_auc"]:.3f}.')+evidence('The useful story is the trade-off',f'At the validation-locked threshold ({C["locked_threshold"]:.3f}), held-out precision is {pct(C["locked_result"]["precision"])} and recall is {pct(C["locked_result"]["recall"])}. Most flagged sessions still do not purchase.')+evidence('A better question for the next experiment','Can a helpful intervention create incremental purchases without harming customer experience? Prediction alone cannot answer that. Test a defined intervention prospectively with a randomized control group.')
cm=f'''<ol><li>Retain all {fmt(C['raw_sessions'])} sessions. UCI describes one user per session; identical feature rows are therefore retained rather than assumed to be duplicate users.</li><li>Exclude PageValues because its relationship with transaction value presents leakage risk. Use Month only to define splits. Engagement features make this a retrospective session analysis.</li><li>Train on observed months before October, choose between logistic regression and random forest on October average precision, and hold out November–December.</li><li>Set a score threshold at the 80th percentile of October validation scores. Refit the selected model on pre-test months and evaluate that threshold unchanged on the holdout.</li><li>Report ranking quality, calibration error, precision, recall, and shuffled-feature importance. The dashboard allows exploratory threshold comparisons.</li></ol>'''
cl='''<p>Session totals and exit metrics may only be complete after a visit ends. This is not a deployable early-session targeting model. Raw event timestamps are unavailable, and month-level ordering cannot establish exact event chronology.</p><p>Holiday-season shift, class imbalance, uncalibrated probabilities, and modest precision limit operational use. No causal lift, revenue gain, or saved marketing cost is claimed. The threshold slider explores held-out outcomes; tuning on it would require a new holdout.</p><p>Next: collect point-in-time event features, calibrate on a separate validation period, monitor drift, and run a controlled intervention. Source: <a href="https://doi.org/10.24432/C5F88Q">Sakar &amp; Kastro (2018), UCI Online Shoppers</a>, CC BY 4.0.</p>'''
case(projects[2],conversion_dash,cf,cm,cl,[('holdout-predictions.csv','Held-out scores and outcomes (CSV)'),('session-summary.csv','Observed session groups (CSV)'),('results.json','Evaluation and feature importance (JSON)'),('analysis.py','Model training and evaluation (Python)')])
